    "question": "Part I: You purchase a company at Year 0 with $100M EBITDA at a 10x multiple, using 4x leverage. You sell at 11x and EBITDA grows to $200M. You also have $200M in net cash at exit. Calculate MoM and approximate IRR over 5 years.Part II: Rank the impact of operating improvements, multiple expansion, and debt paydown.",
    "answer": "IB Vine Select Leveraged Buyouts (LBOs) Part I: Purchase = $1B ($100M x 10). Debt = $400M, equity = $600M. Exit Enterprise Value = 11 x $200M = $2.2B. Exit Equity Value = EV - Net Debt. Since Net Cash is $200M, Net Debt is -$200M. Exit Equity = $2.2B - (-$200M) = $2.4B. MoM = $2.4B / $600M = 4.0x. Roughly 33% IRR over 5 years. Part II: Greatest impact usually comes from EBITDA growth, then multiple expansion, then debt paydown. However, in this specific case, the massive deleveraging (generating $600M in value) outweighs the 1-turn multiple expansion ($200M in value).",
