    "question": "What causes circularity in an LBO model, and how can you address it?",
    "answer": "Reported in Greenhill & Co. Interview SA 2026, New York office, M&A group, Superday Leveraged Buyouts (LBOs) Circularity in an LBO model typically arises from the interdependence between interest expense and cash flow available for debt repayment. Interest expense depends on debt balances Debt balances depend on cash available for repayment Cash available for repayment depends on interest expense (since interest is subtracted from EBITDA to get to Net Income or FCF) To address this, you can: Use a circular reference breaker (e.g., Excel iteration): File → Options → Formulas → Enable iterative calculation. Excel will converge on a stable solution through iteration. Use a plug or hardcode for interest: Create a simplified interest schedule, using prior-period debt balances to calculate interest (this removes forward-looking dependency). INterestInterest becomes an input, not a circular output.",
