    "question": "LBO Case StudyAssume a financial sponsor plans to acquire a company via LBO and has two options:Option 1: Raise a $100mm bank loan and use 100% of the company's cash flow available for debt service to pay down the outstanding loan balance each year.Option 2: Raise a $100mm bond and do not repay any of the bond until maturity at the end of 5 years; excess cash is retained on the balance sheet.In both options, the interest rate of the debt is 10%, and the company generates $35 million of cash flow available for debt service (before interest and principal payments) per year over the next five years. EBITDA is $20mm each year. The purchase is funded with $100mm of debt and $50mm of equity (EV = $150mm), and the sponsor exits at the end of Year 5 at 10x EBITDA.QuestionsAssuming the same initial investment, which option would likely deliver a higher return to the private equity firm at the end of 5 years? Why?If the sponsor could earn 7% interest on cash balance in OPTION 2, would that change your answer?In both options, what could the sponsor do to boost its returns?If the sponsor chooses Option 2, and invests $50mm of equity into the deal, calculate the sponsor's return on investment expressed as a multiple of money invested (i.e. 1x, 2x).",
    "answer": "LBO Case Study Assume a financial sponsor plans to acquire a company via LBO and has two options: Option 1: Raise a $100mm bank loan and use 100% of the company's cash flow available for debt service to pay down the outstanding loan balance each year. Option 2: Raise a $100mm bond and do not repay any of the bond until maturity at the end of 5 years; excess cash is retained on the balance sheet. In both options, the interest rate of the debt is 10%, and the company generates $35 million of cash flow available for debt service (before interest and principal payments) per year over the next five years. EBITDA is $20mm each year. The purchase is funded with $100mm of debt and $50mm of equity (EV = $150mm), and the sponsor exits at the end of Year 5 at 10x EBITDA. Questions Assuming the same initial investment, which option would likely deliver a higher return to the private equity firm at the end of 5 years? Why? If the sponsor could earn 7% interest on cash balance in OPTION 2, would that change your answer? In both options, what could the sponsor do to boost its returns? If the sponsor chooses Option 2, and invests $50mm of equity into the deal, calculate the sponsor's return on investment expressed as a multiple of money invested (i.e. 1x, 2x). Reported in Guggenheim Partners Interview Leveraged Buyouts (LBOs) Question 1 Option 1 would deliver a higher return. Amortizing debt lowers interest expense over time (since the principal balance drops), whereas Option 2 pays 10% on the full $100mm every year. Lower interest expense means more equity value. Question 2 If you could earn 10% on cash → indifferent. If lower than 10% on cash (e.g., 7%) → Option 1 more attractive (you save 10% on repaid debt while earning <10% on cash). If higher than 10% on cash → Option 2 more attractive. Question 3 Increase leverage, reduce costs/increase cash flow, pay out annual dividend to sponsor, etc. Question 4 Answer: 4.5x MOIC Calculation: Exit EV: 10x * $20mm EBITDA = $200mm Cash build: $35mm * 5 = $175mm less interest $10mm * 5 = $50mm → $125mm Ending debt: $100mm Exit equity value: $200mm + $125mm − $100mm = $225mm → MOIC = $225mm / $50mm = 4.5x",
